\f1\b\fs32 \cf0 1. Utah: The Benchmark for Consistency\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gis.utah.gov/products/sgid/elevation/lidar/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Utah Geospatial Resource Center (UGRC)}} prioritizes highly processed 
\f1\b 0.5-meter resolution DEMs
\f0\b0  for its populated valleys. An 8 points/\\(\\text\{m\}^\{2\}\\) density means individual landscape features like retaining walls, small outbuildings, ditch lines, and sidewalk curbs are easily distinguishable when planning parcel updates. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mngeo.state.mn.us/committee/3dgeo/acquisition/faq.html"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/6f2f3ae997ec4fd3a6f0b31d1ce8bdcb"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}]\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs32 \cf0 2. Oregon: Massive Scale, High Quality\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oregon.gov/dogami/lidar/pages/index.aspx"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Oregon Lidar Consortium (DOGAMI)}} features some of the highest-density rural data in the country. Because Oregon uses intense laser penetration to punch through thick forest canopies, their raw point clouds are incredibly detailed. The resulting bare-earth maps strip away dense vegetation perfectly, revealing historical logging grades, precise landslide risks, and highly accurate stream channels. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://opentopography.org/news/lidar-datasets-virginia-california-montana-illinois-oregon-and-utah-available"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/UTGeologicalSurvey/posts/new-utah-geological-survey-mapping-uses-high-resolution-lidar-to-reveal-previous/1300943782079529/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/NOAADigitalCoast/posts/we-love-seeing-the-landscape-come-to-life-with-lidar-data-check-out-this-view-fr/1306223121536662/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://digitalcommons.calpoly.edu/context/theses/article/1379/viewcontent/A_Comparison_of_Lidar_Generated_Channel_Features_with_Ground_Surv.pdf"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 4}}]\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs32 \cf0 3. California: Specialized for Wildfire and Canopy Architecture\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 California's approach focuses heavily on 
\f1\b vegetation structure
\f0\b0  rather than just flat bare-earth terrain. The groundbreaking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wildfiretaskforce.org/california-unveils-first-ever-statewide-lidar-maps/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 WERK Initiative dataset}} maps tree heights, canopy density, and underlying "ladder fuels". While the raw source data features sub-meter precision, the publicly accessible 
\f4\i statewide
\f0\i0  maps are heavily downsampled into 
\f1\b 10-meter and 30-meter grids
\f0\b0  to handle the computing strain of over 100 million acres of data. For raw QL1 resolution, users must target specialized local captures via the USGS LiDAR Explorer or OpenTopography. [{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://opentopography.org/news/march-2026-data-releases"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 1}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wildfiretaskforce.org/california-unveils-first-ever-statewide-lidar-maps/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 2}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usgs.gov/3d-national-topography-model/expired-fy26-3dep-data-collaboration-announcement-dca"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 3}}]\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

