\fs32 \cf0 To create a highly accurate 3D 
\f1\b digital twin of homeowners' parcels
\f0\b0 , you need to extract three distinct structural elements: the bare terrain (topography), the building envelopes (structures), and the vegetation (canopy).\
Because your project requires millimeter-to-centimeter precision to render realistic houses, fences, and trees, you must use specific data formats from each state's repository.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs40 \cf0 1. The Ideal Data Pipeline for Digital Twins\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf0 To build the twin, 
\f1\b do not use pre-processed Digital Elevation Models (DEMs)
\f0\b0 . DEMs are flat "bare-earth" images that intentionally strip away buildings and trees. Instead, you must download the 
\f1\b Raw LiDAR Point Clouds (
\f5\fs28 .LAZ
\f1\fs32  or 
\f5\fs28 .LAS
\f1\fs32  formats)
\f0\b0 .\
Once downloaded into your 3D or GIS engine (like Unreal Engine, Blender, QGIS, or ArcGIS Pro), you will isolate the data using standard LiDAR classification codes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Class 2 (Ground):
\f0\b0  Generates your high-resolution terrain mesh.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Class 6 (Buildings):
\f0\b0  Extrudes exact 3D rooflines, building heights, and footprints.\
\ls10\ilvl0
