\f1\b\fs40 \cf0 3. Overcoming the "Parcel Alignment" Challenge\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf0 A common hurdle when building parcel digital twins is that 
\f1\b legal parcel boundaries (GIS shapefiles) do not always align perfectly with physical features in a LiDAR scan
\f0\b0  due to differing coordinate projection systems.\
To ensure a homeowner's fence line perfectly matches their digital twin boundary:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Reproject all downloaded parcel shapefiles and LiDAR points into the exact same 
\f1\b Local State Plane Coordinate System
\f0\b0  (e.g., 
\f4\i NAD83 / Utah Central
\f0\i0  or 
\f4\i NAD83 / California Zone 3
\f0\i0 ).\
\ls14\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do not use global Web Mercator (WGS84) for calculations, as it introduces geometric distortion at the property level.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
