\f1\b\fs40 \cf0 2. The Step-by-Step Data Pipeline in Rails\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs28 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs32 \cf0 Step A: Ingest and Crop via PDAL\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 You cannot load an entire city\'92s LiDAR file for a single house. When a homeowner signs up, Rails takes their parcel polygon from PostGIS and uses 
\f1\b PDAL
\f0\b0  to crop out just that specific property from the larger state 
\f2\fs28 .LAZ
\f0\fs32  file.\
You can execute a PDAL pipeline script directly inside a Rails background job (
\f2\fs28 Sidekiq
\f0\fs32 ) using standard system calls:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf0 ruby\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs28 \cf0 # app/jobs/process_parcel_twin_job.rb\
class ProcessParcelTwinJob < ApplicationJob\
  queue_as :default\
\
  def perform(homeowner_id, parcel_polygon_wkt)\
    # Define a PDAL JSON pipeline to crop the state LAZ file to the homeowner's parcel\
    pdal_pipeline = \{\
      "pipeline": [\
        "/data/state_sources/salt_lake_city_2023.laz",\
        \{\
          "type": "filters.crop",\
          "polygon": parcel_polygon_wkt # Passed from PostGIS\
        \},\
        "/data/output/twin_#\{homeowner_id\}.laz"\
      ]\
    \}.to_json\
\
    File.write("pipeline_#\{homeowner_id\}.json", pdal_pipeline)\
    \
    # Execute the open-source C++ tool from Rails\
    system("pdal pipeline pipeline_#\{homeowner_id\}.json")\
  end\
end\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Use code with caution.\
